# UI — review the interface, not the architecture
## Choose a visual direction when requested
When the user names a design-pack slug, load
`assets/design-packs/{slug}.md` directly. When the user gives only a mood or
asks for a design pack, read `assets/design-packs/catalog.md`, recommend at
most three suitable packs with a reason, or select one and state it when the user
asked for implementation. Do not enumerate all 40 packs or load every pack.
Apply the selected pack's color, typography, layout, component, motion, and
**do-not-use** rules. The pack defines visual requirements, not a model, runtime,
or framework. Use the active web workflow's supported tools and verify responsive
behavior and rendering separately. A pack never relaxes the accessibility,
focus, form, hydration, locale, or interaction requirements below.
The design-pack specifications are MIT-licensed original/adapted visual
principles from <https://design-diversity.vercel.app>. They do not authorize
copying a product's trademark or logo.
The rules are not ours. They come from **Vercel's Web Interface Guidelines**
(<https://github.com/vercel-labs/web-interface-guidelines>, MIT) and live
verbatim in `references/web-interface-guidelines.md`. `NOTICE` records the
version and what we changed.
**What we changed is one thing: we vendored them.** Vercel's own skill fetches
the rules over the network on every run. Ours reads a file. The cost of that
choice — staleness — and the three reasons for it are in `NOTICE`.
## What this reviews
103 rules across 16 areas. The ones people forget:
| Area | The rule that gets missed |
| --- | --- |
| Accessibility | icon-only buttons need `aria-label`; interactive elements need keyboard handlers |
| Focus States | focus must be visible and must not be removed without a replacement |
| Forms | every control needs a label; errors must be announced, not just coloured |
| Hydration Safety | anything time- or locale-dependent differs between server and client |
| Locale & i18n | text expands; dates, numbers and plurals are not string concatenation |
| Anti-patterns | a flagged list — these are findings, not preferences |
Read `references/web-interface-guidelines.md` for the full set. **Do not
paraphrase it from memory** — the rules are specific and the file is short.
## How to run a review
1. Take the files or glob the user named. If none, ask — do not guess a scope.
2. Read `references/web-interface-guidelines.md`.
3. Check each file against every area. The anti-pattern list is a checklist,
not a vibe.
4. Report as `file:line` with the rule, one finding per line. Terse over
grammatical — the guidelines say so and they are right for this output.
## What this is not
| Question | Skill |
| --- | --- |
| Is the React code fast or well-composed? | the `react` skill |
| Does the change deserve to merge? | the `audit` skill |
| Should I fix it, and in what order? | the `cleanup` skill |
| Write the failing test first? | the `tdd` skill |
`ui` answers **"does this interface behave correctly for the person using it"**
— accessibility, input, state, locale. It says nothing about performance or
component design. When a finding is really a composition problem, name it and
hand it to the `react` skill; do not fix it here.
## Limits
- **The rules are a snapshot.** Upstream may have moved. `NOTICE` carries the
pinned commit; `upstream` carries the refresh procedure.
- **No machine checks this.** Every rule is applied by reading. There is no
linter here, so a review that says "no findings" means a person or a model
read the files — not that a tool passed.
- **Framework-agnostic by construction, but written against web platform
behaviour.** React Native is out of scope.
